winewayland: kill switch for the initial viewport destination (NEUTRON_VIEWPORT_INIT=0)

A build tester reports Premiere's splash and other dialogs rendering in the TOP-LEFT corner from
11.10-69 onward -- every launch, 5/5 counted, not sporadic.

Only two patches separate 68 from 69, and on the evidence neither should be able to cause it:

  * zzzzzzzzzzx (keyboard foreground guard) -- ruled out BY EXPERIMENT on the tester's machine:
    NEUTRON_KBD_FOREGROUND=2 restores the old unconditional behaviour (proven reaching the code by
    a wlsetfg count that goes 0 -> 1), and the splash stayed top-left.
  * zzzzzzzzzzy (this one) -- runs only inside wayland_client_surface_create(), which is called
    only from opengl.c:109 and vulkan.c:50. No #32770 dialog gets a client surface on either
    machine; both machines' logs were checked, with channel liveness proven in the same log.

Everything else was eliminated too: only bin/neutron's version string changed in the shipped stack;
win32u.so is byte-identical between the two releases; user32.dll differs by exactly 6 bytes at
offsets 137-139 and 217-219 (PE timestamp + checksum), so the wine base did not move; the compositor
placement policy is unset on both machines; both enumerate phantom WaylandOutputN entries, so that
is normal driver behaviour and not a divergence; and the splash frame is a plain xdg_toplevel with
an identical structure and identical logical size on both.

⚠️ So the evidence says "neither patch" and the operator says "it started with 69". Rather than keep
arguing from the code about what can reach a window, this adds the switch that settles it. This
patch calls NtUserGetWinMonitorDpi() and NtUserGetClientRect() from inside client-surface creation
during early GL setup -- win32u calls made from the driver, which this project has a standing
lock-order rule about (round-34 ABBA). "Cannot touch it directly" is not "cannot affect it", and I
have no basis for ruling out a global side effect on monitor/DPI state.

NEUTRON_VIEWPORT_INIT=0 disables the initial destination, restoring 11.10-68 behaviour for this
patch. Default on = shipped behaviour.

  * splash centres with =0  -> this patch is the cause; the fix is to obtain the scale without
    calling into win32u from here.
  * splash still top-left   -> the runtime is exonerated entirely and the cause is in the install
    or the prefix, not the engine.

⛔ Note what this patch actually fixes, so it is not casually reverted: without it the first
fullscreen entry is ~1.7x oversized, because the viewport destination is applied ~9 ms after the
first frame is already on screen.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -2754,6 +2754,31 @@
     .present = wayland_client_surface_present,
 };
 
+/* NEUTRON kill switch for the initial viewport destination (NEUTRON_VIEWPORT_INIT=0 disables).
+ *
+ * Added 2026-08-31 because a build tester saw Premiere's splash and other dialogs render in the
+ * TOP-LEFT corner starting with 11.10-69, every launch, 5/5 counted. Only two patches separate 68
+ * from 69 and neither should be able to do that: the keyboard-foreground guard was ruled out by
+ * experiment on their machine, and this one runs only inside wayland_client_surface_create(),
+ * which is called only from opengl.c and vulkan.c -- and no #32770 dialog gets a client surface on
+ * either machine.
+ *
+ * ⚠️ "Cannot touch it DIRECTLY" is not "cannot affect it". This code calls
+ * NtUserGetWinMonitorDpi() and NtUserGetClientRect() from inside client-surface creation, during
+ * early GL window setup. Those are win32u calls made from the driver, and this project has a
+ * standing lock-order rule about exactly that (round-34 ABBA). A switch is cheaper than another
+ * argument from the code about what can and cannot reach a window.
+ *
+ * If disabling this centres the splash, this patch is the cause and the fix is to obtain the scale
+ * without calling into win32u here. If it does not, the runtime is exonerated entirely and the
+ * cause is in the install or the prefix, not the engine. */
+static BOOL neutron_viewport_init(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_VIEWPORT_INIT"); on = (e && e[0] == '0') ? 0 : 1; }
+    return on;
+}
+
 struct wayland_client_surface *wayland_client_surface_create(HWND hwnd)
 {
     struct wayland_client_surface *client;
@@ -2801,7 +2826,7 @@
      * The scale here is derived the same way the surface's own scale is (raw monitor DPI / 96),
      * so this matches what reconfigure_client will compute; it is a correct value, not a guess,
      * and is refreshed by the normal path a few ms later regardless. */
-    if (client->wp_viewport)
+    if (client->wp_viewport && neutron_viewport_init())
     {
         UINT dpi = NtUserGetWinMonitorDpi(hwnd, MDT_RAW_DPI);
         RECT r = {0};
